Oh my gosh, this is a shit show.

12
00:00:16.948 --> 00:00:17.108
Done.

13
00:00:17.408 --> 00:00:23.515
So on a daily basis, theoretically, I'm losing 60% of my productivity just by switching between two different types of tasks at once.

14
00:00:23.695 --> 00:00:23.855
Yeah.

15
00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:30.713
And over two hours a day is the average now of how much time is being wasted bouncing between screens and different tasks.

16
00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:35.679
If we stay this distracted, we will live a life that we don't like living.

17
00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:36.981
So what is in this book?

18
00:00:37.081 --> 00:00:38.042
We're going to go through the books.

19
00:00:38.182 --> 00:00:41.126
I'm going to teach you all the science for productivity and human performance.

20
00:00:41.226 --> 00:00:48.312
Because I know that the default settings of all of our technology are designed by people like me to maximize engagement.

21
00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:52.676
For example, the infinite scroll is like going to a buffet and just opening your mouth.

22
00:00:52.756 --> 00:00:55.959
And there's just an assembling line of food and candy just going straight in.

23
00:00:56.119 --> 00:00:57.019
There's no backstop.

24
00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:00.282
But we can use reverse neuroscience to reverse this entire thing.

25
00:01:00.462 --> 00:01:02.264
Where's the first place to stop?

26
00:01:02.384 --> 00:01:03.925
First, put your phone in gray.

27
00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:12.608
Research from my lab shows grayscale makes the phone less neurologically stimulating and we saw on average people spent one hour less on their phones every single day.

28
00:01:12.628 --> 00:01:13.609
That's seven hours a week.

29
00:01:13.849 --> 00:01:16.450
Next is choosing long form over short form content.

30
00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:23.453
If we have too much short form, your attention span is shot, you literally feel shittier and your brain operates at a lower level,

31
00:01:23.753 --> 00:01:26.714
I can see in your productivity toolkit, there is a clock.

32
00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:29.895
Yeah, this is a huge, huge game changer in terms of productivity.

33
00:01:29.935 --> 00:01:34.976
But also there is a focus sprint to help people to do up to two times as much as they would otherwise do.

34
00:01:35.216 --> 00:01:41.617
And we can talk about biological chronotypes that dictate the ideal time you should be doing your focus sprints.

35
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:46.539
But also I have another amazing thing to strengthen your mind and make you better at focusing.

36
00:01:46.699 --> 00:01:48.019
So I'm going to teach you all this stuff.

37
00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:49.079
Okay, so let's get into it.

38
00:01:49.199 --> 00:01:51.680
So the first thing we ought to really focus in on is...

39
00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:55.125
Guys, I've got a favour to ask before this episode begins.

40
00:01:55.446 --> 00:02:01.532
The algorithm, if you follow a show, will deliver you the best episodes from that show very prominently in your feed.

41
00:02:01.812 --> 00:02:07.017
So when we have our best episodes on this show, the most shared episodes, the most rated episodes, I would love you to know.

42
00:02:07.177 --> 00:02:10.000
And the simple way for you to know that is to hit that follow button.

43
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.362
But also, it's the simple, easy, free thing that you can do to help us make the show better.

44
00:02:14.382 --> 00:02:19.925
And I would be hugely grateful if you could take a minute on the app you're listening to this on right now and hit that follow button.

45
00:02:20.165 --> 00:02:22.807
Thank you so, so, so much.

46
00:02:27.389 --> 00:02:35.774
Dr. Saha Youssef, what is the mission that you are on if you had to try and capture it in a couple of sentences?

47
00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:39.476
And why does it matter to the people that are tuning in and listening right now?

48
00:02:40.356 --> 00:02:58.009
The mission I am on is to, first and foremost, bring science education to the world in a way that allows people to work more in line with how their brains and their bodies actually work best.

49
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:05.835
This is my philosophy, that there are fundamental laws of how the brain and the body actually work.

50
00:03:06.416 --> 00:03:08.798
And I always liken these things to the laws of physics.

51
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.550
There are laws of physics.

52
00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:11.851
We don't question the laws.

53
00:03:12.811 --> 00:03:13.291
Gravity.

54
00:03:13.751 --> 00:03:16.412
I don't ever wake up one morning and go, you know what, I want to do away with it.

55
00:03:16.852 --> 00:03:23.893
And I know that there are fundamental laws of biology, but we blissfully ignore these laws of biology.

56
00:03:24.253 --> 00:03:36.936
We don't teach kids when they're in school at an early age how their brains work so that they might be able to study a little bit easier, you know, find flow, actually be able to manage things like stress, physiological stress.

57
00:03:39.877 --> 00:03:41.479
In my area of expertise is productivity.

58
00:03:41.539 --> 00:03:44.081
We focus so much on what.

59
00:03:44.582 --> 00:03:45.522
What's the deliverable?

60
00:03:45.883 --> 00:03:47.024
What's the long-term goal?

61
00:03:47.044 --> 00:03:47.744
What's the annual goal?

62
00:03:47.764 --> 00:03:48.485
What's the quarterly goal?

63
00:03:48.505 --> 00:03:49.746
What, what, what, what, what, what, what?

64
00:03:50.066 --> 00:03:50.827
But not the how.

65
00:03:52.088 --> 00:03:54.490
How should we go about getting these things done?

66
00:03:54.670 --> 00:04:00.335
What is the best way, in fact, that a human being might sit down and have an innovative thought?

67
00:04:00.656 --> 00:04:03.979
Like, truly, like, what's, like, let's, for example, the neuroscience of innovation?

68
00:04:05.025 --> 00:04:05.785
We're all obsessed.

69
00:04:05.805 --> 00:04:06.766
I'm from Silicon Valley.

70
00:04:06.806 --> 00:04:08.326
We're obsessed with sort of innovation.

71
00:04:08.486 --> 00:04:10.187
But we're doing that with this.

72
00:04:10.207 --> 00:04:11.267
With our brains.

73
00:04:11.507 --> 00:04:11.967
With our brains.

74
00:04:12.487 --> 00:04:13.728
This is a biological system.

75
00:04:14.128 --> 00:04:18.129
Isn't it a good idea to take a step back and learn how the system actually works?

76
00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:22.530
I just feel like there's so much friction with how we're working and living today.

77
00:04:23.131 --> 00:04:27.232
And I would say the number one thing that seems to be getting in the way of all of this is...

78
00:04:28.132 --> 00:04:36.886
at the moment, technology, which is such a shame because it's also the most amazing piece of, I would say, modern human invention.

79
00:04:37.746 --> 00:04:38.666
You said innovation there.
